# Python workspace environment — copy to .env and fill in.
#
# A secret never lives in this file, except the throwaway local database URLs below.
# `artloupe.config` resolves every secret the same way, first match wins:
#   1. The macOS keychain, when APP_ENV is local and the secret's keychain account is set.
#   2. A mounted file: <NAME>_FILE when set, otherwise /run/secrets/<name> (Docker's default).
#   3. Outside production only, a throwaway value such as the local Supabase database URL.
# A secret is never read from the process environment, so exporting ANTHROPIC_API_KEY does
# nothing. Paid provider keys are always refused in CI (CI=true or APP_ENV=ci).
#
# Not every setting below can live in this file. Each section heading says where it is read:
#   - "this file": python/.env or python/.env.local, read from a fixed path.
#   - "this file, from python/": python/.env only, and only when the process starts in python/,
#     as `uv run --directory python` does.
#   - "shell only": the process environment. Writing it here does nothing.

# --- Model providers (this file) ---------------------------------------------
# Anthropic, the Studio Director's model. Locally the key is the keychain item at
# (ARTLOUPE_KEYCHAIN_SERVICE, ARTLOUPE_ANTHROPIC_KEYCHAIN_ACCOUNT). Store it once with:
#   security add-generic-password -s art-loupe -a <account> -w
# Leaving `-w` last with no value makes `security` prompt for the key, so it never lands in
# shell history.
# ARTLOUPE_KEYCHAIN_SERVICE=art-loupe
# ARTLOUPE_ANTHROPIC_KEYCHAIN_ACCOUNT=<account of the keychain item>
#
# OpenAI (learning embeddings) and Pexels (Get Inspired) follow the same pattern, each under its
# own keychain account. Pexels is optional: with no key, that provider reports itself unavailable.
# ARTLOUPE_OPENAI_KEYCHAIN_ACCOUNT=<account of the keychain item>
# ARTLOUPE_PEXELS_KEYCHAIN_ACCOUNT=<account of the keychain item>
#
# Off a developer machine (Docker, CI, production), mount each key as a file instead. These
# path variables are read from the process environment; the defaults are shown.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/anthropic_api_key
# OPENAI_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/openai_api_key
# PEXELS_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/pexels_api_key

# --- Environment (this file) -------------------------------------------------
# local | docker | ci | production. Gates behaviour that must never run outside development:
# docker, ci and production read no env file and never touch the keychain, production never
# accepts a throwaway secret, and ci never resolves a paid key. docker is what compose.yaml
# sets; otherwise it behaves like local. Any other value fails validation rather
# than falling back to local. A deployment sets it in the process environment.
# APP_ENV=local

# --- Database (this file, from python/) --------------------------------------
# Local Supabase, from `pnpm supabase status`. Required only by the backends that
# read it; the defaults are in-memory so tests and CI stay hermetic. These URLs carry a
# password, so they are secrets: the local values here are throwaway, and production ignores
# them and reads only a mounted file (DATABASE_URL_FILE, ARTLOUPE_RUN_LOG_DATABASE_URL_FILE).
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres

# LangGraph checkpoints. "memory" (default) keeps them in-process; "postgres" stores them in
# the `langgraph` schema, which a resumed run needs. The pool size is per process.
# ARTLOUPE_PERSISTENCE=postgres
# ARTLOUPE_CHECKPOINT_POOL_MAX=10

# Where runs and their progress events are recorded. "memory" (default) keeps them in the
# process; "postgres" writes `public.runs` and `public.run_events`, which the studio streams.
# The writes go through a login that can assume `artloupe_run_recorder`. Unset, the URL falls
# back to DATABASE_URL, which works locally. A deployment mounts a dedicated login as a file.
# ARTLOUPE_RUN_LOG=postgres
# ARTLOUPE_RUN_LOG_DATABASE_URL=<login that holds artloupe_run_recorder and nothing else>

# --- Auth: artloupe.auth (this file, from python/) ---------------------------
# Python never handles a user's credential: the Next.js apps authenticate against Supabase
# directly and forward the access token, which these settings verify. See ADR 0002.
#
# SUPABASE_URL is shared with the apps. The anon key is public by design; the
# service-role key is NEVER read by this library.
# SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<local anon key from `pnpm supabase status`>

# Expected `iss` claim, only when it differs from <SUPABASE_URL>/auth/v1. Inside Docker the
# agent reaches Supabase at host.docker.internal while tokens still name 127.0.0.1, so
# scripts/docker/up.sh sets this. Leave it unset on the host.
# SUPABASE_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:54321/auth/v1

# Expected `aud` claim. Supabase issues "authenticated" for signed-in users.
# ARTLOUPE_JWT_AUDIENCE=authenticated
# How long a fetched JWKS document is reused before refetching, in seconds.
# ARTLOUPE_JWKS_CACHE_SECONDS=600
# Timeout for fetching the JWKS document, in seconds.
# ARTLOUPE_AUTH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# Per-request timeout when the agent calls Supabase as the artist. Generous, because an
# original can be 25 MB (FR-101).
# ARTLOUPE_ARTIST_API_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# The legacy HS256 signing secret, ONLY for a Supabase project that still issues HS256 tokens.
# It is a secret, so it is never read from this file or the environment: mount it as
# /run/secrets/supabase_jwt_secret, or point SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET_FILE at it. Leave it unset
# locally: a local `supabase start` signs with an asymmetric key (ES256) and publishes it at
# /auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json, which verification fetches whenever this is unset. Mounting
# it anyway forces HS256 verification, and every locally issued token then fails with a 401.
# When present, verification is symmetric, meaning this process holds material that can also
# SIGN tokens.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/supabase_jwt_secret

# --- Metering and run guards: artloupe.metering (this file, from python/) ----
# Where a finished run's per-node ledger goes. "memory" (default) keeps it in the
# process, which is correct for tests and useless to operations; "postgres" writes
# `public.run_node_metrics`, which is what the cost dashboard reads. It uses DATABASE_URL.
# ARTLOUPE_METERING=postgres

# The caps on one graph run. Every one of these is configuration rather than a
# constant so a demo can tighten a ceiling and show the hard stop firing.
#
# Supersteps in one run. 25 is LangGraph's own default, passed explicitly.
# ARTLOUPE_RUN_RECURSION_LIMIT=25
# Executions of any single node in one run — fires earlier than the recursion limit
# and names the node that is looping.
# ARTLOUPE_RUN_NODE_VISIT_LIMIT=10
# Deadline for one run: four model calls, six with a plan revision. A token that expires
# sooner than this is refused before the run starts. NFR-03's p95 45-60s is a target to measure.
# ARTLOUPE_RUN_WALL_CLOCK_SECONDS=300
# The NFR-04 per-project plan budget, input and output tokens together. Chat credits
# (NFR-11) are a separate meter and neither can stop the other.
# ARTLOUPE_PLAN_TOKEN_CEILING=250000

# --- Agent service (shell only) ----------------------------------------------
# Address and port the agent's HTTP service binds. The apps read it as ARTLOUPE_AGENT_URL.
# Loopback by default; the Docker image sets 0.0.0.0 so the other containers can reach it.
# ARTLOUPE_AGENT_HOST=127.0.0.1
# ARTLOUPE_AGENT_PORT=8080
# The Studio Director's model. Configuration rather than a constant, so an eval can compare
# models without a code change.
# ARTLOUPE_DIRECTOR_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Operations cost panel and run health: a login with membership in `artloupe_ops_reader`,
# which can read only the cost ledger and the run event log. The service sets that role on
# every pooled connection, so locally the stack's own DSN reads no more than the role allows.
# Deployed, mount a dedicated restricted login as a file (ARTLOUPE_OPS_DATABASE_URL_FILE),
# never the project-owner DSN or a service-role key. Unset, the /ops endpoints answer 503 and
# the dashboard says the data is unavailable.
# ARTLOUPE_OPS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres

# Get Inspired cache connection: a login that can assume `artloupe_inspiration_cache`. Unset,
# the cache is off and every search goes to the provider. Deployed, mount the restricted-login
# DSN as a file (ARTLOUPE_INSPIRATION_DATABASE_URL_FILE); never a project-owner connection.
# ARTLOUPE_INSPIRATION_DATABASE_URL=<restricted cache-login connection string>

# --- Book-grounded learning (shell only) -------------------------------------
# Absolute directory containing corpus.jsonl and manifest.json; vectors.npy is optional.
# Unset, the agent still starts and the learning endpoint answers 503.
# ARTLOUPE_LEARNING_CORPUS=/absolute/path/to/python/learning-corpus
# ARTLOUPE_LEARNING_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# ARTLOUPE_LEARNING_JUDGE_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# The OpenAI key for embeddings is under Model providers, above.

# --- Planned, not yet read by any code ----------------------------------------
# Retrieval backend: unset will use committed fixtures, and `pgvector` will read the embedded
# corpus from Postgres.
# ARTLOUPE_RETRIEVAL_BACKEND=pgvector
#
# Dev AI-call cache: an opt-in read-through cache over external AI calls, so iterating does not
# re-spend tokens on identical calls. Off by default, and meant to be inert in CI and production.
# Today only `poe clean` knows its default location, python/.cache.
# ARTLOUPE_AI_CACHE=1
# ARTLOUPE_AI_CACHE_DIR=/path/to/cache
#
# Agent log level: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace. The Next.js apps read their own.
# LOG_LEVEL=info
